RQ: When is the company's own signal strong enough to trust, and when is it mostly noise?



Company-specific patterns may work better when:
- the company has substantial loss volume;
- its link ratios are relatively stable across accident years;
- its claims process has been consistent;
- its development pattern differs systematically from peers;
- removing one accident year does not drastically change its selected factors.
Pooling may work better when:
- the company is small;
- only a few matched observations exist;
- one large claim can dominate its triangle;
- link ratios jump dramatically between accident years;
- late-development factors depend on one or two observations;
the company’s own factor estimates are highly sensitive to which years are included.


